# `deno fmt` emits LF and reports a CRLF file as "Text differed by line endings", while this
# machine's git runs with core.autocrlf=true, which hands out CRLF on every checkout. Those two
# fight each other: formatting the tree fixes `deno fmt --check` only until the next checkout
# re-introduces CRLF, which is why the "main.ts + src/ is fmt-clean" note kept going stale.
#
# Pinning eol=lf for TypeScript settles it — the blobs are already stored as LF, so this changes
# nothing in history; it only stops the checkout filter from converting them back to CRLF.
*.ts text eol=lf

# ── Binary artifacts: mark EXPLICITLY, never rely on git's guess ────────────────────────────────
# 66 `.wasm` fixtures are tracked. With core.autocrlf=true git decides text-vs-binary by heuristic:
# it looks for a NUL byte within the first 8000 bytes. A WebAssembly binary's 4-byte magic ends in
# one, so the heuristic has always won and nothing is corrupt today — verified 2026-08-27, 40 files
# sampled, zero blob-vs-worktree mismatches. **That is luck holding, not a guarantee.** The guess
# reads a prefix, and a mangled `.wasm` surfaces as a malformed module far from its cause.
*.wasm binary

# ── WAT / WAST: the same LF pin as *.ts, for the same reason ────────────────────────────────────
# Every blob is already LF — verified 2026-08-27, 30 `.wast` and 20 `.wat` sampled, none containing
# CR — so this changes NOTHING in history. It only stops the checkout filter handing back CRLF.
# The `.wast` corpus is vendored upstream bytes that must not be rewritten; pinning eol is precisely
# how to leave them alone, as against reformatting them.
#
# ⚠️ Adding a rule does NOT re-materialise files already in the working tree — existing line endings
# survive until a file is deleted and checked out again. `git add --renormalize .` fixes the INDEX,
# not the working tree, and it re-applies conversion to EVERY tracked file: run it here and it also
# rewrites `LICENSE*` and `.github/FUNDING.yml`, whose blobs still carry CRLF. Those are a separate
# decision and do not belong in a change about WASM artifacts. Verify with
# `git check-attr text eol binary -- <file>`, never by eyeballing the file.
*.wat  text eol=lf
*.wast text eol=lf
